# HigherOrderCO's runtimes in hyperactivity

Three of Higher Order Company's systems run as hyperactivity modules and are emitted to C. Each
is checked against its upstream on the upstream's own tests.

| module | what it is | upstream | result |
|---|---|---|---|
| `lib/hvm2` | HVM2: interaction combinators under HVM2's rule table (LINK CALL VOID ERAS ANNI COMM OPER SWIT); u24, i24 and f24 numbers; safety analysis; readback; IO | HigherOrderCO/HVM `src/hvm.rs`, `src/run.c` | 20 of 20 run snapshots that have a program (`tests/snapshots/run__*`) |
| `lib/ieee32` | IEEE binary32 on words: exact results rounded to nearest; Rust's casts, decimal reading and shortest display; correctly rounded transcendentals in 2^-256 fixed point | used by `lib/hvm2` and `lib/bend` | carried by hvm2's f24 snapshots and bend's F32 tests |
| `lib/hvm4` | HVM4: the Interaction Calculus runtime behind SupGen, with its parser, lazy book, reduction (every interaction), collapser and printer | HigherOrderCO/HVM4 `src/hvm.c` | 217 of 217 tests (`devs/test`) |
| `lib/bend` | Bend: bend2's parser, pattern flattener, machine, checker and verdict, plus the IO lane's scheduler, files, TCP/UDP, and the JS lane's U32/Nat/F32/String/Array operations | HigherOrderCO/bend `bend2/bend.ts`, `main.ts`, `comp.ts` | 1724 of 1872 tests print the same bytes as `bun bend2/main.ts` |
| `lib/emit-slices` | emits a module's C in pieces, one view each, so no view holds the whole emission | — | used to build `lib/bend` |

Two of bend's IO tests (`io/chan_buffer_drain`, `io/http_fetch_eof`) depend on timing and can fail
when the suite runs in parallel; they pass run alone.

`lib/bend` also checks all five checker benches (`bench/checker`) and prints what bun prints.

## Taking the files out

Every file under `ports/` is a document in the store's root: its text is its module's texts in
order. From the repository's top, this writes them all to disk:

    r=$(cat store/ROOT/root); p=$(printf ports/ | od -An -tx1 | tr -d ' \n')
    grep "^$p" store/$r/root | while read x h; do
      f=$(printf "$(echo $x | sed 's/../\\x&/g')"); mkdir -p "$(dirname "$f")"
      for t in $(cat store/$h/module); do cat store/$t/text; done > "$f"
    done; chmod +x ports/*.sh ports/*.py ports/hvm2/*.py

## Build and test

    ./ports/build.sh bend          # ./bend  (BEND_DIR=<bend checkout>/bend2, or pass BASE.bend second)
    ./ports/build.sh hvm4          # ./hvm4
    ./ports/build.sh hvm2          # ./hvm2
    ./ports/test-bend.sh <bend checkout> ./bend
    ./ports/test-hvm4.sh <HVM4 checkout> ./hvm4
    ./ports/test-hvm2.sh <HVM2 checkout> ./hvm2

`build.sh` compiles with `cc -O2`; the results above hold under gcc and clang. Building `bend`
takes about 15 minutes, one view per 1000 functions.

## Sources

`lib/bend` and `lib/hvm4` are generated. Edit their sources, not the modules:

- `ports/bend/*.src` and `ports/hvm4/*.src` are the sources, written in a do-notation over flat
  memory.
- `ports/h4gen.py` expands that do-notation into the continuation-passing form the modules hold.
- `ports/topo.py` orders the generated definitions.
- `ports/regen.sh bend|hvm4` rewrites a module from its sources.

`lib/hvm2` and `lib/ieee32` are written directly, except hvm2's interaction loop (`hr-go`), which
`ports/hvm2/gen_go.py` prints; the module holds that output verbatim.

Regenerating names its fresh binders by a counter in the store, so a regenerated module's hash
depends on the store it is written into. Two regenerations of the same sources differ only in
those names.

## What does not run in `lib/bend`

- **113 tests** call the test's own foreign JavaScript. Running them needs a JavaScript
  interpreter plus the JS lane's value conversions.
- **20 tests** call `Process.run`, mostly `rm -f` to clean up. The C runtime has no fork, exec or
  unlink call.
- **9 tests** use Window or Audio.
- **2 tests** run out of memory on long channel loops. The RAM heap has no collector; its objects
  carry no header saying where their pointers are.
- **4 tests** depend on compile-time layout checks of the C and JS compilers.
- Socket calls return -1 without errno. A failed connect is reported as ECONNREFUSED, and a
  failed bind or listen as EADDRINUSE.

## How `lib/bend` runs

**Code and memory.** The sources are continuation-passing code over one flat memory of words.
Every load and store passes the memory base on through the value it produced, so nothing after
a step can run before it.

**Reclaiming closures.** The runtime has one mark slot: `w-sys 900` marks and `w-sys 901`
rewinds. Outer regions rewind their closures when they finish:
- a book item (`b2-pitem`)
- a definition's check (`b2-bvstep`)
- an IO step (`b2-iostep`)

An inner mark would move the outer region's rewind past the closures made before it. So inner
regions mark only when they run long:
- a wnf run marks after its first 8 batches of 512 steps;
- a flatten scan marks after the first 4096 scans in an item.

Answers leave a region through RAM registers.

**Bounding the C stack.** A continuation-passing step is a C call. A command returns to the
runtime's trampoline, so `w-now` is issued as a no-op:
- in the parser,
- at each flatten level,
- in check and infer,
- every 256 `term_higher` steps.

**The machine.** It is bend.ts's wnf over an explicit frame stack in RAM. The frame kinds:

| kind | frame |
|---|---|
| 1 | APP |
| 2 | MAT |
| 3 | VAR (share-cell write-back) |
| 4, 5 | MNA, MNB |
| 6 | a native's argument evaluated, then the call retried |
| 7 | IO-lane call by value: the argument evaluated, then back to the lambda with its lhs |

Native arguments are evaluated on this stack, never by a nested run. A nested run would take
the mark slot from the batch around it.

**Natives (`b2-intrnames` / `b2-intrcodes`).**

| codes | operations | lane |
|---|---|---|
| 1–37 | U32, Nat | every lane |
| 40–84 | F32 | IO lane only, as the JS lane's natives |
| 90–93 | String | IO lane only, as the JS lane's natives |
| 100–118 | Array | IO lane only, as the JS lane's natives |

The checker evaluates only by Base's definitions, as bend.ts does.

**Text, arrays and sockets.**
- **Strings.** A String literal holds an array of UTF-16 units, or a rope `[ROPE, left, right,
  length]` made by `String.append`. A rope is flattened the first time it is read, and the
  result is kept as `[FWD, array]`. A code point past the scalars is packed as U+DC00 and three
  units, as `char_new` packs it.
- **Arrays.** An Array on the IO lane is a literal of kind 4 over a RAM array of cells. A match
  on it sees `ALeaf` or `ANode` halves.
- **Sockets.** A socket operation that would wait parks as a scheduler timer of kind 3, and is
  tried again every millisecond while the queue is empty.

**Open: the RAM heap has no collector.** Its objects carry no header saying where their pointers
are. A collector needs that layout first, and is what long-running programs here are missing.
